            <ArticleTitle>Mesenteric vein thrombosis due to factor V Leiden gene mutation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1059-62</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Mesenteric venous thrombosis is a rare cause of acute abdominal pain that may be the result of coagulation abnormalities.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Four consecutive patients with mesenteric venous thrombosis underwent haematological evaluation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">All four had activated protein C resistance resulting from a single mutation in the gene coding for coagulation factor V. Three had surgery; in one patient the diagnosis was made by ultrasonography. One of the patients who had surgery died but the other three survived and were treated with long-term anticoagulation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Activated protein C resistance may be an important pathogenetic factor in primary mesen-teric vein thrombosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
